is pipette  step that was dipped into the mounting  media we attached it  into all three chambers not just this  chamber  we attached it and then  we ran the sample  through the instrument and any aerosols  that were created would hopefully stick  to that mounting media  we also tested that the aerosols did  stick to mounting media with a spray  bottle and they did and then we could  pull out the pipette tip  and  rub them rub the tip over a slide  and then look at that slide in the  microscope  so this was the system for testing the  inside all three chambers of the  cartridges  to test the exterior of the cartridge  this is  more  along the lines of what you do for  conventional cell sorting so we use the  cyclix d cassettes  and we ran the samples for 10 minutes  like they do um  also for conventional sorting and we  tested two locations above the cartridge  so this one location  here above two of those filters and then  a second location above the third filter  here  so there were three interior  samples that we took  two exterior samples that we took  and then we also  um  did another sampling outside  of the closed sword chamber door  and this vent here that i mentioned  earlier  the results were  encouraging if not exciting  i mean  exciting in some senses and boring and  others but  we detected no aerosols of any size we  tested all three sizes with this method  the 50 nanometers 100 nanometers and one  micron beads  we didn't detect them at any size  from inside the positive or the negative  chambers  we weren't able to in the end so far we  haven't been able to make a  determination for the input chamber and  i'll explain why in the next slide but  we didn't detect any of the positive and  negative chambers  so that's like the pipet test the  pipette tip  we were also unable to detect beads of  any size outside of the cartridge  and we were not able to detect them  outside of the instruments  so  that was very encouraging we have  some work left to do  one is to determine the lower limit of  detection  um  we know that after looking at the slides  for hours so we were not able to see any  beads  um but we're working on  ensuring that we're looking at the right  plane  the right focal plane in the microscope  so that we're definitely not missing any  of those 50 nanometer beads because  they're hard to see  so  we're very confident that if aerosols  are generated it's very few  because we couldn't detect any  but we still need to go back and  create that threshold that lower limit  in addition  we  have to  optimize our  bead loading system  because when we loaded the beads we  accidentally touched the sides  unknowingly of the lure lock and so when  we took the pipette tips out they  brushed against the sides and  contaminated the sample  so we're working on a funnel system  to add the beads without touching the  sides and then we'll be able to get  those results  we need to repeat this for multiple lots  and also test the high speed cartridges  this should be really interesting  because they do operate at a higher  pressure than the regular cartridges  if we do  um detect beads say in the input chamber  aerosols in the input chamber  or after we do more replicates we detect  beads  in one of them  our plan is to think about adding a  layer of mineral oil  to the top  let's see  of the cartridge and go way back like  here  such that or even the top of the  the sorter chamber wherever we need it  if we need it would be to add a layer of  mineral oil  to prevent aerosols from escaping  through the top which i think  is a really interesting idea  and that would  also create a safer sorting scenario if  we had to  so i just wanted to give a big thank you  to megan varnado and katelyn didier  they are  specialists in  my  full core and our flowcore at tulene  they did a lot of this work  we have  had as i mentioned in the first slide  it's been a journey  trying to we thought in the beginning  that this would be very very easy and  it wasn't it was like i said like  impossible to clog  impossible to get the pressure high  um  lots of difficulties making this thing  break it does not break easily  and they have been very patient and  we've tried so many things to get to  this place so big thank you to both of  them for their persistence  and then from bill tenney daryl megan  ellie elaine matt duet crystal  extremely helpful extremely  knowledgeable  and i'm sure my conversations with them  will be continuing as we continue  testing  and my boss midi core from tulane and  cecily from the confocal core who made  the proprietary mounting media that we  were able to use in pipette sips because  all of the commercial ones that we  tested  did not work they all basically dripped  into the sample  and then just here this is not a very  good picture but this is our new title  in the bsl3  and we're not allowed to have cameras in  there our cell phones in there so it's  it's a picture taken from outside the  bsl3 actually but  we are very excited to start working on  it in this space  so thank you for your time and i'll be  happy to take any questions  thank you matt and marissa for your  informative presentations we will now  start the live q a portion of the  webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question box located on the far left of  your screen we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  i work at a lab  at biotech near tulane university  can i use your flow core services  yes we collaborate with external people  investigators from different  universities and companies so if you're  interested in using it definitely get in  touch  fantastic thank you and the second  question is what types of cells  have your flow core sorted on the pitot  on our side we've sorted  a wide variety from  like granulomas from lungs infected with  tuberculosis  as well as mucosal samples  and pbmc  and  lymph node  so we can do all kinds of tissues  fantastic  do you have any  lab sorting samples from covid19  patients right now  yes we we do have labs supporting topic  19 patients um they'd be uh you know  they're treated just as any other  hazardous samples  and there is a publication if anybody's  interested  feel free to  comment in the chat and we can  get that to you after the webinar thanks  fantastic  the next question we have is um we would  like to sort  gfp positive bacteria is that possible  with this system  what decontamination procedures would i  need  so yes this that is positive possible  with  the title and when it comes to  decontamination uh it's really simple  all you need to do is dispose of the  cartridge so as as you saw in the  presentations uh everything the entire  sample is enclosed in the cartridge  there's no fluidics throughout the  actual instrument to contaminate it so  again throwing away that cartridge would  be the  cleaning and decan decontamination  process  fantastic well thank you so much matt  and marissa do you have any final  comments for our audience today  i would say thanks to everybody that is  attending um live today and in the  future recorded and thanks so much for  marissa and her team for all the the  hard work uh on this uh this project  awesome thank you guys again  for your time today and for your  important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor miltoni biotech for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before i go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email  when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have 